/// \defgroup grp_w1021_stringbase_assign_edit StringBase construction, assignment and editing
/// \ingroup grp_common_services
/// \brief The StringBase class and its members that copy, assign, format
///        and edit a string: set, operator=, concat from a character
///        source, format and format_va, trim, the case conversions,
///        removeLastChar, getBufferForRead and clear.
///
/// StringBase is the template that both of the engine's string classes
/// are built on: AsciiString over `char` and UnicodeString over 16-bit
/// UTF-16 code units. \ref sub_common_services_strings describes the two
/// classes and how text converts between them; the StringBase class page
/// in this group describes the shared buffer, its limits and its locking.
///
/// This group lists the public members that change a string's text:
/// - **Assignment**: operator=, set from another string, set from a
///   substring, and set from a character source.
/// - **Appending** from a character source (concat).
/// - **Formatting**: format and the two format_va overloads.
/// - **Editing in place**: trim, toLower, toUpper and removeLastChar.
/// - **Buffer access**: getBufferForRead and clear.
///
/// The constructors and the peek helper are private, and this guide is
/// built with EXTRACT_PRIVATE=NO, so they have no entries in this group.
/// The StringBase class page summarises them under "Private constructors
/// and peek".
///
/// Members outside this group include the comparison and search members
/// (compare, compareNoCase, startsWith, endsWith, find, reverseFind and
/// their overloads and NoCase variants), nextToken, small accessors such as
/// str, getLength, isEmpty and getCharAt, and the remaining set and concat
/// overloads (from one character, a C string, a counted C string or
/// another string), which the members here call or mirror.
///
/// \par Where the code comes from
/// - Zero Hour has no StringBase. Its AsciiString and UnicodeString are
///   two separate classes, and the BFME games turned AsciiString into this
///   template. Where an entry below compares with Zero Hour, it compares
///   with Zero Hour's AsciiString (`Common/System/AsciiString.cpp`).
/// - Most bodies here follow Open-BFME-1's StringBase
///   (`game/Libraries/Source/string/StringBase.cpp` in the BFME 1
///   reconstruction). BFME 2 adds the character-source overloads and the
///   constructor and set that take a C string with a start and a length;
///   BFME 1's StringBase declares neither.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Nothing in this group is data-driven, but every INI value, map name,
/// localized string and file path the engine handles passes through it,
/// so its hard-coded limits apply to game data. The StringBase class page
/// lists them.

/// \class StringBase
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_assign_edit
/// \brief Reference-counted, copy-on-write string template shared by
///        AsciiString (`char`) and UnicodeString (16-bit code units).
///
/// A StringBase object is a single pointer to a heap buffer, or null for
/// an empty string. The buffer starts with a small header (a reference
/// count, a 16-bit length and a 16-bit capacity) followed by the
/// characters and a terminating zero. Copies share one buffer and only
/// raise its reference count; a member that changes the text first makes
/// sure the buffer is not shared and is large enough, copying it if
/// needed. AsciiString and UnicodeString keep this one-pointer layout;
/// \ref sub_common_services_strings explains how each is built on it.
///
/// \par Ownership and lifetime
/// - The constructors and the destructor are private. AsciiString and
///   UnicodeString are friends of the class, and their constructors call
///   the StringBase constructors (for example, AsciiString's constructor
///   from a C string calls StringBase's), so other code never creates a
///   bare StringBase.
/// - The destructor and clear release the buffer: the reference count
///   drops by one, the last owner frees the buffer, and the pointer is set
///   to null. That work happens in releaseBuffer, which every member that
///   replaces a buffer calls too.
/// - New buffers come from the engine's `str` heap
///   (\ref sub_memory): the allocation passes that heap's id, the
///   multi-character constant <code>'str'</code>, to STLport's byte
///   allocator as its hint.
///
/// \par Invariants
/// - An empty string normally has no buffer. A string whose last
///   character is removed, or that is set to zero characters, releases its
///   buffer rather than keeping an empty one. getBufferForRead with a
///   length of 0 is one way to get a buffer of length 0. isEmpty treats
///   both forms as empty: it is true when there is no buffer or the stored
///   length is 0.
/// - The text is always followed by a terminating zero, and the capacity
///   counts that terminator. A member writes in place only when the buffer
///   is unshared and its capacity is greater than the length it needs.
/// - The length is stored, not measured, so a string can hold zero
///   characters in the middle of its text. str returns a pointer to the
///   characters, or to a static empty string when there is no buffer, so
///   its result is always a zero-terminated C string; with an embedded
///   zero, that C string is shorter than the stored length.
/// - A pointer returned by str, or by the search members find and
///   reverseFind, points into the buffer. Treat it as valid only until the
///   string is next changed or destroyed: a change can rewrite the text in
///   place or release the buffer.
///
/// \par Hard-coded limits
/// - A buffer, header and terminator included, may not exceed 32,767
///   bytes, checked before the size is rounded up to a multiple of four.
///   That allows at most 32,758 `char` characters or 16,378 UTF-16 code
///   units in one string (an append can hit the limit sooner; see
///   StringBase::concat). A member that would need more throws an `int`
///   with the value 1, as BFME 1 does. Zero Hour's AsciiString has the
///   same ceiling (its `MAX_LEN`) but throws `ERROR_OUT_OF_MEMORY`.
/// - Length and capacity are 16-bit fields.
/// - format and format_va build their text in an 8,192-character stack
///   buffer.
///
/// \par Locking
/// Each instantiation has its own process-wide lock object: one for
/// `char` strings and one for the 16-bit strings, each holding a Win32
/// critical section and created on first use. The copy constructor,
/// set from another string, and releaseBuffer take that lock while they
/// change a reference count. When the lock object is destroyed at process
/// exit it deletes its critical section and sets a flag, and from then on
/// those members skip the critical section, so strings released later in
/// shutdown never touch the deleted one. Zero Hour's
/// AsciiString instead updates the count with an interlocked increment or
/// decrement; BFME 1 has the BFME 2 scheme.
///
/// Only the reference-count changes are guarded. The members that edit
/// text in place (set from text or a character source, concat, trim, the
/// case conversions, removeLastChar, getBufferForRead) check the count
/// without taking the lock, so one string object must not be changed from
/// two threads at once.
///
/// \par Private constructors and peek
/// This guide is built with EXTRACT_PRIVATE=NO, so the private
/// constructors and peek have no entries of their own. In short:
/// - The default constructor leaves the pointer null. Every other
///   constructor except the copy constructor also starts with no buffer
///   and forwards its arguments to the matching set: one character, a C
///   string (measured with `strlen` or `wcslen`; a null pointer counts as
///   empty), a C string with a count, a substring of a C string or of
///   another string, or a character source.
/// - The one-character constructor passes the address of its character and
///   a length of 1 to the counted set, so a zero character gives a string
///   of length 1 whose only character is zero, not an empty string.
/// - The constructor from a C string with a start and a length is new in
///   BFME 2, as is the character-source constructor. BFME 1's StringBase
///   has the substring constructor from another string, but neither of
///   these two.
/// - The copy constructor copies no characters: it points at the source's
///   buffer and raises its reference count under the lock for its
///   character type (see Locking). An empty source gives an empty copy.
///   Zero Hour's AsciiString copy constructor raises the count with an
///   interlocked increment and takes no lock.
/// - peek returns the first character of the buffer with no null check,
///   so it may only be called when the string has a buffer. str checks
///   that first and returns a static empty string otherwise.
///
/// \par Character sources
/// CharSource is a BFME 2 interface that neither Zero Hour nor BFME 1
/// has: an abstract source of characters that reports how many it will
/// supply and copies them into a buffer it is given. set, concat and a
/// constructor accept one, and the counted-C-string set and concat wrap
/// their pointer and length in a temporary CharSource and take the same
/// path. Some engine helpers that append text to an AsciiString supply
/// their own CharSource implementation.
///
/// \par Debug hooks
/// debugIgnoreLeaks and the private validate are empty in BFME 2. Zero
/// Hour's AsciiString::validate is a debug-build consistency check on the
/// buffer (reference count, allocated size and string length).
///
/// \par Where it is used
/// Everywhere text is handled. Through AsciiString and UnicodeString:
/// INI parsing (INI::getNextAsciiString, INI::parseAsciiString), chunk
/// files (DataChunkTableOfContents::read), the map cache
/// (MapCache::findMap, MapCache::writeCacheINI), save-game map paths
/// (GameState::realMapPathToPortableMapPath), preference files
/// (UserPreferences::load, SkirmishPreferences), and the Apt and GameSpy
/// front ends. Most entries in this group name some of their callers.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - A single string that would exceed the 32,767-byte buffer limit
///   throws instead of being truncated, and nothing in StringBase catches
///   it. Text from data files (INI values, map and string-table text) is
///   subject to this limit once it is in a string.
/// - Formatted text longer than 8,192 characters is not handled safely
///   (see StringBase::format_va).
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - The wide instantiation uses 16-bit `unsigned short` code units
///   (UTF-16), which is what `wchar_t` is on Windows. A port to a platform
///   with a 32-bit `wchar_t` must keep 16-bit units or convert at every
///   boundary that passes them to the C runtime.
/// - The 16-bit length and capacity and the 32,767-byte ceiling are part
///   of the layout that AsciiString and UnicodeString share. Raising the
///   limit means changing the header for both.
/// - The set from another string takes the lock and then calls
///   releaseBuffer, which takes the same lock again from the same thread.
///   Win32 critical sections allow that; a port's replacement lock must be
///   recursive too.
/// - Formatting, whitespace tests and case conversion call the Microsoft C
///   runtime (`_vsnprintf`, a counted `vswprintf`, `isspace`,
///   `tolower`, `toupper` and their wide forms). A port needs equivalents
///   with the same return-value behaviour.

/// \fn StringBase::StringBase(T c)
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_assign_edit
/// \brief Constructs a string of one character.
///
/// Starts with no buffer and passes the address of its own parameter and
/// a length of 1 to the counted set, so the result has its own buffer
/// holding c. AsciiString's constructor from a single `char` calls it.
///
/// \note Worked from the bodies: a zero character still gives a string of
///       length 1 whose only character is zero, not an empty string.
/// \param c The character.
/// \note Private, so this page appears only when private members are
///       extracted.
/// \see StringBase::StringBase(const T *, int)

/// \fn StringBase::StringBase(const T *str, int len)
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_assign_edit
/// \brief Constructs a string from the first len characters of a C string.
///
/// Starts with no buffer and hands both arguments to the counted set,
/// which copies exactly len characters into a new buffer, whether or not
/// the source contains a zero before that. A len of 0 gives an empty
/// string with no buffer. AsciiString's constructor from a pointer and a
/// count calls it.
///
/// \param str The characters to copy. Must be readable for len
///            characters.
/// \param len The number of characters to copy.
/// \note Private, so this page appears only when private members are
///       extracted.
/// \see StringBase::StringBase(const T *)

/// \fn StringBase::StringBase(const T *str)
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_assign_edit
/// \brief Constructs a string from a zero-terminated C string.
///
/// Measures str (`strlen` for `char`, `wcslen` for the 16-bit form; a null
/// pointer counts as empty) and passes the pointer and length to the
/// counted set. AsciiString's constructor from a C string calls it, and
/// so do many functions that build an AsciiString from a C string, among
/// them INI::prepFile, INI::loadDirectory and the INI math-expression
/// parsers.
///
/// \param str The text to copy, or null for an empty string.
/// \note Private, so this page appears only when private members are
///       extracted.
/// \see StringBase::StringBase(const T *, int)

/// \fn StringBase::StringBase(const T *str, int start, int len)
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_assign_edit
/// \brief Constructs a string from a substring of a C string.
///
/// Starts with no buffer and calls the matching set, which clamps start
/// and len to the source's measured length. AsciiString's constructor
/// with the same three arguments calls it. BFME 1's StringBase has no such
/// constructor.
///
/// \param str   The source text, zero-terminated, or null for empty.
/// \param start Index of the first character to take; see the set
///              overload for how out-of-range values are clamped.
/// \param len   Number of characters to take.
/// \note Private, so this page appears only when private members are
///       extracted.
/// \see StringBase::set(const T *, int, int)

/// \fn StringBase::StringBase(const StringBase< T > &src, int start, int len)
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_assign_edit
/// \brief Constructs a string from a substring of another string.
///
/// Starts with no buffer and calls the matching set. AsciiString's
/// constructor with the same arguments calls it, and so do
/// PlayerList::validateTeam and MapMetaData::getDescription, which build
/// such a substring directly.
///
/// \param src   The source string.
/// \param start Index of the first character to take.
/// \param len   Number of characters to take.
/// \note Private, so this page appears only when private members are
///       extracted.
/// \see StringBase::set(const StringBase< T > &, int, int)

/// \fn StringBase::StringBase(const CharSource< T > &src)
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_assign_edit
/// \brief Constructs a string from a character source.
///
/// Starts with no buffer and calls the character-source set. Like the
/// CharSource interface itself, this constructor is new in BFME 2.
///
/// \param src The source that supplies the characters.
/// \note Private, so this page appears only when private members are
///       extracted.
/// \see StringBase::set(const CharSource< T > &)

/// \fn StringBase::StringBase(const StringBase< T > &src)
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_assign_edit
/// \brief Constructs a copy that shares src's buffer.
///
/// No characters are copied: the new string points at src's buffer and
/// the buffer's reference count goes up by one, under the lock for this
/// character type (see the StringBase class page). An empty src gives an
/// empty copy. The first later edit to either string copies the buffer.
///
/// AsciiString's and UnicodeString's copy constructors call it, and so do
/// the many functions that pass or return strings by value, for example
/// INI::getNextAsciiString, INI::loadDirectory and W3DDisplay::init.
///
/// \param src The string to copy.
/// \note Zero Hour's AsciiString copy constructor raises the count with
///       an interlocked increment and takes no lock.
/// \note Private, so this page appears only when private members are
///       extracted.
/// \see StringBase::set(const StringBase< T > &)

/// \fn StringBase< T > & StringBase::operator=(const StringBase< T > &src)
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_assign_edit
/// \brief Makes this string share src's text.
///
/// Calls StringBase::set with src and returns this string. AsciiString's
/// and UnicodeString's own assignment operators call that set directly.
///
/// \param src The string to copy.
/// \return This string.
/// \see StringBase::set(const StringBase< T > &)

/// \fn void StringBase::set(const StringBase< T > &src)
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_assign_edit
/// \brief Makes this string share src's buffer, releasing its own.
///
/// Under the lock for this character type, and only when src is a
/// different object: releases this string's buffer (freeing it if this was
/// the last reference), then points at src's buffer and raises its
/// reference count. Assigning a string to itself does nothing.
///
/// Callers include AsciiString::operator= and UnicodeString::operator=,
/// INI::parseAsciiString and INI::parseQuotedAsciiString (which store a
/// parsed value into an AsciiString field), SubsystemInterface::setName,
/// ScienceStore::getNameAndDescription and GameEngine::init.
///
/// The private copy constructor shares a buffer the same way; the
/// StringBase class page summarises it.
///
/// \param src The string to share.
/// \note releaseBuffer takes the same lock again while it is held here;
///       see Remastering on the StringBase class page.
/// \see StringBase, StringBase::clear

/// \fn void StringBase::set(const T *str, int start, int len)
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_assign_edit
/// \brief Replaces the text with a substring of a C string, clamped to the
///        source's length.
///
/// Measures str (a null pointer counts as empty), clamps the requested
/// range, and passes the result to the counted set.
///
/// \par Clamping
/// With n the measured length:
/// - If start + len is negative, or start is at or past n, the string is
///   cleared (its buffer released).
/// - A negative start moves to 0 and shortens len by the same amount, so
///   the range still ends at start + len.
/// - A range that runs to or past n is cut to end at n.
///
/// A range that is empty after this normally clears the string, because
/// the counted set releases the buffer for a length of 0 (but see the note
/// below).
///
/// The substring constructor with the same arguments is its only direct
/// caller. BFME 1's StringBase has no such set.
///
/// \param str   The source text, zero-terminated, or null.
/// \param start Index of the first character to take; may be negative.
/// \param len   Number of characters to take.
/// \note Worked from the bodies: the counted set does nothing when the
///       pointer it is given is the start of this string's own buffer, and
///       this set passes it str plus the clamped start. So when this
///       string's own str() is passed and the range survives the first
///       clamping rule with a clamped start of 0, the text is left
///       unchanged: it is neither shortened nor, when the clamped length is
///       0, cleared. Both the `char` and the 16-bit forms behave this way.
/// \see StringBase::set(const StringBase< T > &, int, int)

/// \fn void StringBase::set(const StringBase< T > &src, int start, int len)
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_assign_edit
/// \brief Replaces the text with a substring of another string, clamped
///        to its length.
///
/// The same clamping as the C-string overload, against src's stored
/// length instead of a measured one; the characters are then copied with
/// the counted set. The substring constructor with the same arguments
/// calls it.
///
/// \param src   The source string.
/// \param start Index of the first character to take; may be negative.
/// \param len   Number of characters to take.
/// \note Worked from the bodies: the counted set does nothing when the
///       pointer it is given is the start of this string's own buffer, and
///       this set passes it src's characters plus the clamped start. So
///       whenever the range survives the first clamping rule and the source
///       characters start at this string's own buffer, the text is left
///       unchanged instead of being shortened or cleared. That happens with
///       a clamped start of 0 when src is this string, or when src is any
///       string that shares this string's buffer, which is the ordinary
///       copy-on-write state after an assignment or a copy: after
///       <tt>a = b</tt> with b not empty, <tt>a.set(b, 0, n)</tt> with n of
///       0 or more leaves a unchanged. Both the `char` and the 16-bit forms
///       behave this way, and so does BFME 1's StringBase.
/// \see StringBase::set(const T *, int, int)

/// \fn void StringBase::set(const CharSource< T > &src)
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_assign_edit
/// \brief Replaces the text with the characters a character source
///        supplies.
///
/// Asks src for its length. A length of 0 clears the string. Otherwise, if
/// this string's buffer is unshared and its capacity is greater than that
/// length, src writes its characters straight into it. If not, a new
/// buffer is allocated from the `str` heap, sized for the header, the
/// characters and the terminator and rounded up to a multiple of four
/// bytes; src writes into it and the old buffer is released. Either way the
/// stored length is the count src reports having written, and a
/// terminating zero follows it.
///
/// The character-source constructor and concat (when the string is empty)
/// call it. The counted set follows the same steps with its pointer and
/// length wrapped in a temporary CharSource, except that it first returns
/// without changing anything when that pointer is the start of this
/// string's own buffer. New in BFME 2.
///
/// \param src The source that supplies the characters.
/// \exception int Thrown (the value 1) when the new buffer would exceed
///            32,767 bytes.
/// \see StringBase::concat(const CharSource< T > &)

/// \fn void StringBase::concat(const CharSource< T > &src)
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_assign_edit
/// \brief Appends the characters a character source supplies.
///
/// Does nothing when src reports a length of 0. An empty string with no
/// buffer is simply set from src. Otherwise ensureUniqueBufferOfSize makes
/// this string's buffer unshared and large enough for both texts, keeps
/// the current text, and lets src append its characters. When the buffer
/// has to grow for an append, ensureUniqueBufferOfSize makes it at least
/// half as large again as the old capacity, so repeated appends do not
/// reallocate every time.
///
/// The growth is applied before the size check, so appending to a string
/// that already holds more than about two thirds of the maximum length
/// can throw even when the combined text would fit.
///
/// Its known direct callers are small engine helpers that append a C
/// string to an AsciiString through their own CharSource. New in BFME 2.
///
/// \param src The source that supplies the characters to append.
/// \exception int Thrown (the value 1) when the buffer would exceed 32,767
///            bytes.
/// \see StringBase::set(const CharSource< T > &)

/// \fn void StringBase::format(const T *fmt,...)
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_assign_edit
/// \brief Replaces the text with printf-style formatted output.
///
/// Passes fmt and the address of the arguments that follow it, which is
/// the argument list on x86, to StringBase::format_va. AsciiString and
/// UnicodeString have their own format members, which call format_va
/// directly.
///
/// \param fmt The format string, in the C runtime's printf syntax.
/// \see StringBase::format_va(const T *, char *)

/// \fn void StringBase::format_va(const T *fmt, char *args)
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_assign_edit
/// \brief Replaces the text with formatted output, from an argument list.
///
/// Formats into an 8,192-character buffer on the stack, with the C
/// runtime's `_vsnprintf` for `char` strings and its counted `vswprintf`
/// for 16-bit strings, then copies the number of characters the formatter
/// reports with the counted set.
///
/// AsciiString::format (both overloads) calls the `char` form, and
/// UnicodeString::format (both overloads) calls the 16-bit form.
///
/// \param fmt  The format string.
/// \param args The argument list (a `va_list`, which is a `char` pointer
///             with this compiler).
/// \warning The formatter's return value is not checked. Microsoft's
///          `_vsnprintf` returns a negative count when the output does not
///          fit in 8,192 characters (the counted `vswprintf` is assumed to
///          do the same; not verified here), and that count is passed on to
///          the counted set, which does not expect a negative length. Keep
///          formatted text shorter than 8,192 characters.
/// \note Zero Hour's AsciiString::format_va uses a 2,048-character buffer,
///       throws `ERROR_OUT_OF_MEMORY` when the formatter fails, and then
///       copies the buffer as a zero-terminated string.
/// \see StringBase::format, StringBase::format_va(const StringBase< T > &, char *)

/// \fn void StringBase::format_va(const StringBase< T > &fmt, char *args)
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_assign_edit
/// \brief Replaces the text with formatted output, taking the format from
///        another string.
///
/// The same as the C-string overload, using fmt's text (an empty format
/// when fmt is empty). The same 8,192-character limit applies.
///
/// \param fmt  The string that holds the format.
/// \param args The argument list.
/// \see StringBase::format_va(const T *, char *)

/// \fn void StringBase::trim()
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_assign_edit
/// \brief Removes leading and trailing whitespace.
///
/// Does nothing to a string with no buffer. Otherwise:
/// 1. Leading whitespace is skipped. If any was found, the rest of the
///    text is set as the new text with the counted set.
/// 2. If the string still has a buffer, trailing whitespace is removed one
///    character at a time with StringBase::removeLastChar.
///
/// A string that is all whitespace ends up empty with no buffer.
/// Whitespace is what the C runtime's `isspace` (for `char`) or `iswspace`
/// (for 16-bit strings) accepts.
///
/// Callers of the `char` form include UserPreferences::load,
/// SkirmishPreferences::getPreferredMap, the GameSpy configuration and
/// ladder parsers (the GameSpyConfig constructor and parseLadder),
/// GameSpyStagingRoom::generateGameSpyGameResultsPacket and
/// VideoPlayer::getVideo. Callers of the 16-bit form include the
/// SkirmishPreferences constructor, LANAPI::RequestSetName,
/// AptInGameChat::Send and AptSkirmish::OnAddProfileAccept.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's AsciiString::trim follows the same steps, but
///       measures the text with `strlen` instead of the stored length.
/// \par Remastering
/// The `char` form passes each character to `isspace` as a signed
/// `char`, so bytes from 0x80 up (UTF-8 sequences, for example) reach it
/// as negative values. A port should pass them as `unsigned char`.
/// \see StringBase::removeLastChar

/// \fn void StringBase::toLower()
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_assign_edit
/// \brief Converts every character to lower case, in place.
///
/// Does nothing to a string with no buffer. Otherwise the buffer is made
/// unshared first (copied into a new `str`-heap buffer if another string
/// shares it), and then each character up to the stored length goes
/// through the C runtime's `tolower` (or `towlower` for 16-bit strings).
/// Characters are converted one code unit at a time, so multi-byte UTF-8
/// sequences are not treated as characters.
///
/// The `char` form has many callers, among them the AsciiString hash
/// functor in the `rts` namespace (which lowercases before hashing),
/// W3DModelDrawModuleData::parseLodOptions and other INI field parsers,
/// GameState::realMapPathToPortableMapPath and the other save-game map
/// path conversions, MapCache::findMap, MapCache::loadUserMaps,
/// ControlBarSchemeManager::findControlBarScheme and GameEngine::init.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's AsciiString::toLower copies the text into a
///       2,048-character stack buffer with `strcpy`, converts it up to the
///       first zero, and sets the result. BFME 2 converts the stored text
///       in place, so it neither stops at an embedded zero nor depends on
///       a fixed buffer size.
/// \exception int Thrown (the value 1) if copying the buffer would exceed
///            32,767 bytes.
/// \see StringBase::toUpper

/// \fn void StringBase::toUpper()
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_assign_edit
/// \brief Converts every character to upper case, in place.
///
/// The same steps as StringBase::toLower, with `toupper` (or `towupper`).
/// HotKeyManager::executeHotKey calls the `char` form. Zero Hour's
/// AsciiString has no such member.
///
/// \exception int Thrown (the value 1) if copying the buffer would exceed
///            32,767 bytes.
/// \see StringBase::toLower

/// \fn void StringBase::removeLastChar()
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_assign_edit
/// \brief Removes the last character.
///
/// Does nothing to a string with no buffer. Otherwise the buffer is made
/// unshared first, as in StringBase::toLower. Then, if more than one
/// character remains, the last one is replaced by the terminator and the
/// length drops by one; if the string had one character (or none), its
/// buffer is released and the string becomes empty.
///
/// StringBase::trim calls it for each trailing whitespace character.
/// Other callers of the `char` form include Win32Mouse::initCursorResources,
/// W3DRadar::buildTerrainTexture, MapCache::loadUserMaps, GameInfo::setMap
/// and LadderPreferences::loadProfile; callers of the 16-bit form include
/// DisplayString::removeLastChar and LANAPI::RequestGameCreate.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's AsciiString::removeLastChar measures the text with
///       `strlen`, and a string reduced to no characters keeps an empty
///       buffer. BFME 2 releases it.
/// \exception int Thrown (the value 1) if copying the buffer would exceed
///            32,767 bytes.
/// \see StringBase::trim

/// \fn T * StringBase::getBufferForRead(int len)
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_assign_edit
/// \brief Sets the length to len and returns the writable buffer, so the
///        caller can read text straight into the string.
///
/// "For read" means "to read into": callers fill the buffer from a file
/// or a calculation instead of building a temporary first.
///
/// If this string's buffer is unshared and its capacity is greater than
/// len, it is kept, existing characters included. Otherwise a new buffer
/// is allocated from the `str` heap and the old one is released without
/// copying its text. In both cases the length becomes len and a
/// terminating zero is written after it. The caller must then write all
/// len characters: in a new buffer, the ones it does not write are
/// undefined.
///
/// Callers of the `char` form include DataChunkTableOfContents::read
/// (which reads the label names of a chunk file), StringBase's nextToken,
/// GetBasePathFromPath and GetBaseFileFromFile; the 16-bit form is used by
/// nextToken, among others.
///
/// \param len The number of characters the caller will write.
/// \return The first character of the buffer, with room for len
///         characters and the terminator.
/// \note Zero Hour's AsciiString::getBufferForRead asks for len + 1
///       characters, asserts that len is positive, and records no length.
///       In BFME 2 the length is set here, and len may be 0.
/// \exception int Thrown (the value 1) when the new buffer would exceed
///            32,767 bytes.

/// \fn void StringBase::clear()
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_assign_edit
/// \brief Empties the string by releasing its buffer.
///
/// Calls releaseBuffer: the buffer's reference count drops, the last owner
/// frees it, and this string is left with no buffer. Usually expanded
/// inline where it is called.
/// \see StringBase::set(const StringBase< T > &)

/// \fn T * StringBase::peek() const
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_assign_edit
/// \brief Returns a pointer to the first character of the buffer.
///
/// A private helper with no null check: it may only be called when the
/// string has a buffer. str uses it after checking that, and returns a
/// static empty string otherwise.
/// \return The first character of this string's buffer.
/// \note Private, so this page appears only when private members are
///       extracted.
